In this lesson we'll look at how to solve simple trig equations that have exactly one solution between zero and two pi. Picture a question like sine of theta equals zero point five. The goal is to undo the trig function and get back to an angle. Here's the recipe. First, find the principal angle, which is the acute angle whose ratio matches. For zero point five, that's thirty degrees, or pi over six radians. Next, ask yourself where that ratio is positive. Sine is positive up the top in quadrants one and two, while cosine is positive in quadrants one and four, and tangent in one and three. Finally, write down every angle in the right quadrants, using the symmetry that the second quadrant angle is pi minus the principal. So our two answers are pi over six and five pi over six. One quick tip, always double check your calculator is in the right units, degrees or radians, before you start. Now let's see it in action.